        return "http://example.com/path2";
    }
}
```
This code defines a simple web server that serves files from two different paths. The `MyController` class is annotated with `@RestController`, which indicates that it handles REST requests.

When you run this application, it will start a web server on port 8080. You can access the web server by navigating to `http://localhost:8080/path1` or `http://localhost:8080/path2`.

To test this, you can use a tool like curl or Postman:
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/path1
```
This should return the contents of `path1`. You can also see that the URL is constructed correctly by looking at the host and path variables.

Alternatively, you can use Java 8 features to create a simple web server without using Spring Boot. Here's an example:
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;

public class WebServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(8080);
        System.out.println("Server is listening on port 8080");
        
        while (true) {
            Socket client = server.accept();
            System.out.println("New connection from " + client.getRemoteAddress());
            
            // Handle the request
            handleRequest(client);
        }
    }

    public static void handleRequest(Socket client) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(client.getInputStream()));
        String line;
        
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println("Received message: " + line);
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a simple web server that listens on port 8080. It accepts incoming connections and handles requests using the `handleRequest` method.

When you run this application, it will start a web server on port 8080. You can access the web server by navigating to `http://localhost:8080/path1` or `http://localhost:8080/path2`.

To test this, you can use a tool like curl or Postman:
```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/path1
```
This should return the contents of `path1`. You can also see that the URL is constructed correctly by looking at the host and path variables.
